========================================================================
3. FRONTEND ROUTING WORKFLOW
========================================================================

Request /
  -> src/app/layout.js creates the root HTML/body.
  -> ThemeProvider wraps the full route tree.
  -> src/app/page.js renders Navbar, Hero, and Footer.
  -> Navbar provides the global theme control and links to /monitor.

Request /monitor
  -> src/app/monitor/page.jsx renders the Monitor client component.
  -> Monitor requests GET /api/incidents/live.
  -> Leaflet is dynamically imported in the browser.
  -> Esri satellite tiles are loaded into the map.
  -> Incident coordinates are rendered as clickable markers.
  -> Selecting a marker shows incident details.
  -> "Download report" navigates to GET /api/reports/:id/pdf.
  -> Browser geolocation can add a temporary user marker.

Request /report
  -> src/app/report/page.jsx renders the Report client component.
  -> Report requests GET /api/incidents.
  -> Filtering and sorting occur in the browser.
  -> Statistics shown on this page are calculated from the filtered list.
  -> A report PDF action navigates to GET /api/reports/:id/pdf.

Request /history
  -> src/app/history/page.jsx renders the History client component.
  -> History requests GET /api/incidents.
  -> Timeline/list filtering and sorting occur in the browser.
  -> Historical summary values are derived from the returned incident list.

Request /printable
  -> PrintableReport concurrently requests:
       GET /api/reports/latest
       GET /api/authorities
  -> The page displays the latest derived report.
  -> GeneratePdfButton downloads GET /api/reports/latest/pdf.
  -> Screen styling follows the selected theme.
  -> Print media styling always uses a white paper background and dark text.

Legacy routes:
  /pages/Monitor -> redirects to /monitor
  /pages/Report  -> redirects to /report
  /pages/History -> redirects to /history

========================================================================
4. GLOBAL DARK-MODE WORKFLOW
========================================================================

Components:
  src/components/ThemeProvider.jsx
  src/components/ThemeToggle.jsx
  src/app/globals.css

On initial load:
  1. ThemeProvider checks localStorage for "ecowatch-theme".
  2. If no saved value exists, it checks prefers-color-scheme.
  3. It applies/removes the "dark" class on the root <html> element.
  4. It sets the browser color-scheme to dark or light.

When the user toggles:
  1. Shared React theme state changes.
  2. Every active page/component receives the same darkMode value.
  3. The root CSS class and native control color scheme update.
  4. The selection is stored as "dark" or "light".
  5. Client-side navigation retains the selection.
  6. A later browser visit restores the selection.

Theme surface coverage:
  - Root body background and foreground
  - Landing navigation and hero typography/borders
  - Monitor header, controls, detail panel, and Leaflet zoom controls
  - Reports header, cards, filters, table, and paging controls
  - History header, cards, filters, timeline/table, and paging controls
  - Printable page screen and print-specific color reset

========================================================================
5. BACKEND REQUEST PIPELINE
========================================================================

Every request enters backend/src/app.js:
  1. CORS accepts CLIENT_ORIGIN (default http://localhost:3000).
  2. express.json() parses JSON request bodies.
  3. The request is routed under /api.
  4. Validation/auth middleware runs where configured.
  5. A controller translates HTTP input/output.
  6. A service performs incident/auth/report logic.
  7. Model helpers normalize response objects.
  8. errorHandler returns a JSON error for thrown failures.

Read workflow:
  Browser -> route -> controller -> service -> in-memory seed array -> JSON response.

Protected write workflow:
  POST /api/auth/login
  -> required email/password validation
  -> authService compares demo credentials
  -> signed 8-hour HMAC token returned
  -> client sends Authorization: Bearer <token>
  -> requireAuth verifies signature and expiry
  -> requireRole allows "admin" or "authority"
  -> requireFields validates the mutation body
  -> incidentService mutates the in-memory array
  -> controller returns the created/updated incident

PDF workflow:
  GET /api/reports/:id/pdf
  -> incidentService finds the incident
  -> Report model derives printable fields
  -> reportPdfService creates a one-page PDF buffer
  -> response uses Content-Type application/pdf
  -> response uses attachment Content-Disposition

========================================================================
6. API ROUTE INVENTORY
========================================================================

GET  /api/health
  Public. Returns service health.

POST /api/auth/login
  Public. Requires email and password.
  Returns a signed token plus a safe user profile.

GET  /api/authorities
  Public. Returns escalation authority objects.

GET  /api/incidents
  Public. Optional query filters: type, severity, status.
  Returns newest-first incident data.

GET  /api/incidents/live
  Public. Returns the first two newest incidents for the map.

GET  /api/incidents/:id
  Public. Returns one incident or HTTP 404.

POST /api/incidents
  Protected. Roles: admin, authority.
  Required fields: type, lat, lng.
  Creates an in-memory incident and returns HTTP 201.

PATCH /api/incidents/:id/status
  Protected. Roles: admin, authority.
  Required field: status.
  Updates an in-memory incident or returns HTTP 404.

GET  /api/reports
  Public. Derives report summaries from all incidents.

GET  /api/reports/latest
  Public. Derives a report from the newest incident.

GET  /api/reports/:id
  Public. Derives one report from an incident ID.

GET  /api/reports/latest/pdf
  Public. Downloads the newest report as a PDF.

GET  /api/reports/:id/pdf
  Public. Downloads one incident report as a PDF.

GET  /api/stats
  Public. Returns total incidents, critical count, affected hectares,
  and average detection confidence.

